WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.200
Since its inception, filmmaking has&nbsp;
been a space of potential danger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.120
In fact, it's often the films with&nbsp;
seemingly the least amount of risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.800
that pose the greatest danger to the actors. From&nbsp;
beloved kids' movies to classic '90s comedies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.240
these are the surprising scenes that&nbsp;
were actually dangerous to film.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.080
Children and adults alike have loved "The&nbsp;
Wizard of Oz" since its release in 1939, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.920
beyond the bright technicolor world was a land of&nbsp;
danger, accidents, and poison. The production of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:36.400
"The Wizard of Oz" has become as legendary as the&nbsp;
film itself, due to its many unfortunate mishaps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.920
The Wicked Witch of the West was accidentally set&nbsp;
on fire when a trap door malfunctioned during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:48.320
scene; the Tin Man got poisoned by his aluminum&nbsp;
heavy makeup; even Terry, the dog playing Toto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.360
wasn't exempt as one of the Winkie Guards&nbsp;
accidentally stepped on her paw and broke it.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.320
But, surprisingly, one of the most dangerous&nbsp;
moments of all was the poppy field scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:04.080
The Cowardly Lion, Tin Man, and Scarecrow stand&nbsp;
above Dorothy, who lies unconscious among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.000
poppies as snow starts falling around them. It&nbsp;
turns out, things weren't so gentle — the fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:17.440
snow was made of asbestos, a toxic carcinogen. The&nbsp;
dangers of asbestos were known by the early 1900s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.000
but that didn't stop producers from dousing&nbsp;
Judy Garland and her co-stars with it.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.520
"Now my beauties, something with poison&nbsp;
in it, I think, with poison in it."

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.280
In spite of its name, "Grease" didn't run&nbsp;
quite as smoothly as producers had hoped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.240
The film's climactic racing scene called for&nbsp;
John Travolta and Dennis Stewart to splash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.680
through water that was stagnant and filled with&nbsp;
bacteria. Also, Jeff Conaway slipped and fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.440
while doing a take of "Grease Lightning," leading&nbsp;
to lifelong back pain and an addiction to opioids.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.200
Ultimately, it was the prom sequence, however,&nbsp;
that proved most dangerous to film. On screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.200
viewers can enjoy a vibrant scene&nbsp;
of bright colors, a dance off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.160
and even John Travolta doing the splits — but&nbsp;
off screen, things weren't quite so fun. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.440
Los Angeles gymnasium where the dance was filmed&nbsp;
didn't have any air conditioning, and doors were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:18.240
closed to control lighting. The location got&nbsp;
so hot — a cool and casual 116 degrees — that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.400
several members of the cast had to be helped&nbsp;
off the set due to heat-related illnesses.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.400
While the standard for filming on location in&nbsp;
the middle of a city is to close down the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.080
that isn't always available when&nbsp;
you're working on a limited budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.440
Such was the case for John Schlesinger, director&nbsp;
of "Midnight Cowboy." In the film, Jon Voight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.040
plays a sex worker who strikes up an unlikely&nbsp;
friendship with Dustin Hoffman's Ratso Rizzo.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.640
The most famous scene of the film&nbsp;
was an accidental one that almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.280
injured Hoffman. Since production didn't have&nbsp;
the money to shut down the streets, they filmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.680
Voight and Hoffman walking and talking in&nbsp;
the middle of real New York City traffic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.760
As they're exchanging dialogue, a cab&nbsp;
driver tried to run through a red light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.120
nearly hitting Hoffman. This led to a genuine&nbsp;
outburst and a classic moment of film dialogue.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.480
"Hey, I'm walkin' here! I'm walking here!"

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.720
1976's "Carrie" continues&nbsp;
to terrify viewers today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.200
with its Stephen King-conceived tale of a&nbsp;
bullied young woman pushed to her breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.080
point. The shoot was intense as Sissy Spacek&nbsp;
was so committed to staying in character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.320
that she isolated herself from her&nbsp;
cast mates, slept in her bloody dress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.280
and even insisted on being buried alive so&nbsp;
she could push her own hand out of the grave.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.040
In spite of all this, it was the&nbsp;
climactic prom scene when Carrie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.440
goes after her tormentors that proved to&nbsp;
be most dangerous. With scenes like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.200
filmmakers have to plan carefully to avoid mishaps&nbsp;
but still, things can happen, especially when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.640
fireman refuses to operate the firehose because&nbsp;
it's too strong and a stuntman does it instead.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.240
PJ Soles, who plays the bully Norma, discovered&nbsp;
this danger first hand when she got sprayed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.960
the ear with a fire hose. The power was so&nbsp;
intense that she suffered from a ruptured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.240
ear drum for six months and the painful moment&nbsp;
is even included in the final cut of the film.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.000
For "The 40-Year-Old Virgin," Steve Carell&nbsp;
decided to go full Method. Not only did he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.680
write the chest waxing scene for himself, but&nbsp;
he also decided to get his chest waxed for real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.320
While normally this isn't so dangerous, the&nbsp;
stakes were unknowingly raised with the hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.040
of Miki Mia, who lied to the casting&nbsp;
directors about her waxing proficiency.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.600
So, while Carell perhaps anticipated some&nbsp;
level of pain, what he didn't expect was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.160
nearly getting his nipples ripped off as Mia&nbsp;
didn't take the important step of putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.760
Vaseline around that area. The scene proved&nbsp;
so painstaking to film that actor Romany Malco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.400
had to leave while Carell gritted his teeth&nbsp;
and followed through with the process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.320
The film's director, Judd Apatow, loves to keep&nbsp;
things real, and Carell's agony was extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.000
real. In the end, it was definitely&nbsp;
worth it, at least for the audience.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.760
"Do you know what, guys? This&nbsp;
is not a good look for me."

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.400
"The Exorcist" has become as famous for its&nbsp;
terrifying set as for its horror on screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.040
It got to the point that people working on&nbsp;
the film actually believed it was cursed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.680
Ellen Burstyn suffered a permanent back&nbsp;
injury while being yanked by a harness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.480
several deaths have been linked to the movie,&nbsp;
including cast members and members of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:28.960
families, an entire set burned down, and a&nbsp;
priest had to be brought in to bless the set.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:35.040
Such a physically demanding film, understandably,&nbsp;
has risks but one of the more dangerous moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.480
came as a result of the director's head-turning&nbsp;
scare tactics. In order to elicit the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:47.200
authentic reactions on screen, William Friedkin&nbsp;
would randomly shoot guns on set. Jason Miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.640
who played Father Karras, got into an altercation&nbsp;
with Friedkin after he fired a gun next to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.240
ear to get a surprise reaction. Miller noted&nbsp;
that as an actor, he doesn't exactly need a gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.760
to be surprised or startled. Friedkin never&nbsp;
denied the claims of using a gun to get a rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:08.640
out of the actors; in fact, he dismissed them&nbsp;
because after all, he was only firing blanks.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.720
Daniel Day Lewis is arguably one of the&nbsp;
most Method actors of all time. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.160
it's spending an entire shoot in&nbsp;
a wheelchair for "My Left Foot,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.360
learning to track and skin animals for "Last&nbsp;
of the Mohicans," or staying in a solitary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.160
prison cell for two nights for "In the Name&nbsp;
of the Father," the man commits to a role.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.920
It's no surprise, then, that he got fully&nbsp;
into character to play one of his most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:38.880
unforgettable characters — violent, angry oilman&nbsp;
Daniel Plainview in 2007's critically acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.680
"There Will Be Blood." Many remember the moment&nbsp;
of Day Lewis lecturing Paul Dano's character…

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.840
"I drink your milkshake!"

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.400
[slurping sound]

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.560
But what viewers may not realize&nbsp;
is that this climactic scene was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.840
actually quite dangerous&nbsp;
to film. Ever the realist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.600
Day Lewis insisted on throwing actual bowling&nbsp;
balls at Dano, who just barely got out of the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.880
The realism certainly added to the moment, helping&nbsp;
create an authentic and iconic film moment.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.200
An action series like "The Hunger Games" requires&nbsp;
a lot of safety planning due to all the stunts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:20.400
but the most dangerous, and unpleasant, scene to&nbsp;
film may surprise you. While Jennifer Lawrence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.600
made headlines after accidentally kicking Josh&nbsp;
Hutcherson in the head and Liam Hemsworth hurt his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:30.880
ankle while filming "Mockingjay Part II," it was&nbsp;
the sewer scene that really put people in danger.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.840
In spite of having a sewer built on a&nbsp;
soundstage, director Francis Lawrence decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:41.760
to do the scene on location in a real sewer.&nbsp;
The water was heated, the ceilings were low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.600
which wasn't ideal for the 6'3” Liam Hemsworth,&nbsp;
and costumes got water-logged, adding at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:53.760
20 extra pounds on top of the actors. Plus,&nbsp;
being in an actual sewer isn't exactly sanitary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.720
which led to a quite miserable three-week shoot&nbsp;
for everyone. But in the end, they got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.760
footage — and in the unique realm of Hollywood,&nbsp;
sometimes that need transcends everything else.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.920
Since its 1971 release, "Willy Wonka and the&nbsp;
Chocolate Factory" has inspired the dreams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.960
of countless kids and grown-ups alike. Willy&nbsp;
Wonka's iconic chocolate river in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.240
stands out as a place of fun&nbsp;
and fantasy. But filming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.240
this candy wonderland wasn't quite&nbsp;
as wondrous as viewers might think.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.280
According to Michael Bollner,&nbsp;
who played Augustus Gloop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:32.000
this dream was a bit of a nightmare. It was&nbsp;
"stinky water," made of 150,000 gallons of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.280
cream, chocolate — and leftover coffee&nbsp;
dumped by folks on set. The stench of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:42.240
the river only got worse over time, as the&nbsp;
cream spoiled during the course of the shoot.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.360
Bollner had to do multiple takes, spending&nbsp;
the day soaked in his smelly clothes. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.560
take required him to go back up into the tube,&nbsp;
where he was stuck with water up to his mouth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.520
The filming of this scene was so problematic&nbsp;
that Peter Ostrum, who played Charlie Bucket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:01.280
noted that OSHA likely had not&nbsp;
approved such working conditions.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.240
It's not just action films that can&nbsp;
prove dangerous for actors and extras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.880
The physical nature of some comedies can&nbsp;
also create opportunities galore for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:15.280
to get injured, which was the case for Mike&nbsp;
Myers and Dana Carvey in "Wayne's World."

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:19.520
Who can hear Queen's "Bohemian Rhapsody"&nbsp;
without thinking of the classic scene from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.080
the first "Wayne's World" movie? Myers fought&nbsp;
to have that song included in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.560
instead of the original pick of Guns n' Roses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:30.560
and it paid off by becoming one of the&nbsp;
most iconic music scenes of all time.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.200
According to Myers, though, it wasn't all fun&nbsp;
and games. The guys in The Mirthmobile got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.160
very into the moment, and director Penelope&nbsp;
Spheeris needed many takes to get proper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:44.960
coverage for all the various angles you see&nbsp;
in the scene. As a result, that scene took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:50.480
almost an entire night to shoot, leading to&nbsp;
some serious neck pain. As Carvey told Rolling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:56.720
Stone in 2015, he was 36 at the time and needed&nbsp;
more than a few Advil to get through the night.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.200
Filming this sequence might have&nbsp;
been a headache for the actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:02.800
but fans everywhere had the same&nbsp;
reaction to the final scene.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:03.840
"Excellent."

00:10:05.166 --> 00:10:08.960
"Ishtar" has gone down in film history&nbsp;
as one of Hollywood's greatest bombs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.920
and trouble plagued it from production to&nbsp;
release. The 1987 adventure comedy stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.960
Warren Beatty, Dustin Hoffman and Isabel&nbsp;
Adjani, and was filmed on location in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:24.400
Morocco. What could go wrong with a handful&nbsp;
of warring egos, intense climate conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:30.400
and high political tensions in North Africa?&nbsp;
Actually, a lot could go wrong — and it did.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.920
The film became infamous for its extravagant&nbsp;
over-spending and director Elaine May's high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:41.200
number of takes. Perhaps the most dangerous scene&nbsp;
was the one where Hoffman lies in the desert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.000
surrounded by vultures. In order to get the&nbsp;
large, frightening birds to fly down around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:52.480
him, production placed raw pieces of meat on&nbsp;
Hoffman's body, just out of frame. Hoffman asked…

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.080
"Are these vultures going to know&nbsp;
where the raw meat ends and I start?"

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:59.840
Unfortunately for him, he had&nbsp;
to do 50 takes to find out.

00:11:01.259 --> 00:11:06.720
"The Conqueror," a 1956 John Wayne film, may be&nbsp;
one of the most surprisingly dangerous films in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.040
history, although you wouldn't know it to&nbsp;
see it. In the early years of the Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:17.200
the US created the Nevada Test Site and in&nbsp;
1951 started testing its nuclear weapons. Due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.160
to the winds, the brunt of the nuclear&nbsp;
fallout was felt in St. George, Utah.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:26.880
When producers of "The Conqueror" decided to film&nbsp;
in St. George, they did so with the assurance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:32.320
the federal government that the nuclear test site&nbsp;
posed no hazard to public health. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.040
every scene of the film that was shot in St.&nbsp;
George proved to be incredibly dangerous.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:44.320
By 1963, director Dick Powell died of cancer&nbsp;
and by 1980, 91 people from the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.760
41 percent, developed the disease. Some say&nbsp;
that Wayne got cancer from this production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:53.440
although The Duke believed it was from&nbsp;
his six-pack-a-day cigarette habit.

